'''belive<sup>2</sup>†''', ''adv.'', orig. ''prep. phr.'' [Now only E. dial., also written ''belyve'', Sc. ''belife'', ''belyve'', ''beliff'', < ME. ''belive'', ''belyve'', ''belife'', ''bilife'', ''blife'', ''blive'', also ''bilifes'', ''bilives'', etc.; sometimes used expletively; prop. two words, ''be live'', ''be life'', lit. by life, i. e., with life or activity; cf. ''alive'' and ''lively''.] '''1.''' With speed; quickly; eagerly.
{{smaller block|<poem>Rise, rise ''bylive'',
And unto batteil doe your selves addresse.</poem>
{{right|''Spenser'', F. Q., II. viii. 18.}}
<poem>Thou schalte haue delyueraunce
''Be-lyue'' at thi list.</poem>
{{right|''York Plays'', p. 231.}}}}
'''2.''' Presently; ere long; by and by; anon: sometimes merely expletive.
{{smaller block|<poem>Twenty swarm of bees,
Whilk all the summer hum about the hive,
And bring me wax and honey in ''bilive''.</poem>
{{right|''B. Jonson'', Sad Shepherd, ii. 1.}}
''Belyve'' the elder bairns come drapping in.
{{right|''Burns'', Cottar’s Sat. Night.}}}}
[Obsolete in both senses, except in Scotch.]

'''belk†''' (belk), ''v. t.'' [E. dial., < ME. ''belken'', the unassibilated form of ''belchen'', ''belch'': see ''belch'', and cf. ''balk''<sup>2</sup>, ''bolk''.] To belch; give vent to.
{{smaller block|Till I might ''belke'' revenge upon his eyes.
{{right|''Marston'', Antonio and Mellida, II. i. 1.}}}}

'''bell<sup>1</sup>''' (bel), ''n.'' [Early mod. E. also ''bel'', < ME. ''bel'', ''belle'', < AS. ''belle'' (= D. ''bel'' = MLG. LG. ''belle''; cf. Icel. ''bjalla'', < AS. ''belle''), a bell. Perhaps connected with ''bell''<sup>2</sup>, ''v.'', roar.] '''1.''' A hollow metallic instrument which gives forth a ringing sound, generally of a musical quality, when struck with a clapper, hammer, or other appliance. {{smaller|Its usual shape resembles that of an inverted cup}}

